KEY CONCEPTS EXPLAINED
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. WEBDRIVERWAIT & EXPLICIT WAITS
   ├─ Problem: Website elements load at different speeds
   ├─ Solution: "Wait for element to exist before clicking"
   ├─ Default wait time: 10-20 seconds
   └─ Prevents: "Element not found" failures

2. XPATH & CSS SELECTORS
   ├─ XPath: "//button[contains(text(),'Register')]"
   │  └─ Finds button by text content
   ├─ CSS: ".product-card a"
   │  └─ Finds link inside product-card div
   └─ Both are ways to identify elements on page

3. JAVASCRIPT EXECUTION
   ├─ Problem: Some clicks don't work with Selenium
   ├─ Solution: Execute JavaScript click
   ├─ driver.execute_script("arguments[0].click();", element)
   └─ Forces the browser to click via JavaScript

4. IFRAMES
   ├─ Problem: Payment gateway in separate frame (iframe)
   ├─ Solution: Switch to iframe context first
   ├─ driver.switch_to.frame(iframe)
   └─ Now can interact with elements inside iframe

5. FIXTURES IN PYTEST
   ├─ Reusable setup/teardown code
   ├─ Marked with @pytest.fixture
   ├─ SETUP: Code before yield
   ├─ TEST: Receives yielded value
   └─ TEARDOWN: Code after yield (cleanup)

6. PAGE OBJECT MODEL (POM)
   ├─ One class per website page
   ├─ Classes store element locators
   ├─ Classes provide action methods
   ├─ Test code remains simple and readable
   └─ Easy to maintain when HTML changes


TOOLS USED DURING EXECUTION
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. SELENIUM WEBDRIVER
   └─> Controls Chrome browser
   └─> Sends commands: navigate, click, type, wait
   └─> Retrieves: elements, URLs, page text

2. PYTHON
   └─> Executes test logic
   └─> Logical decisions: if/else
   └─> Loops and data handling

3. JAVASCRIPT
   └─> Used for scrolling: scrollIntoView()
   └─> Used for clicking: element.click()
   └─> Used for checking: document.readyState

4. CHROME DRIVER
   └─> Bridge between Selenium and Chrome
   └─> Translates Selenium commands to browser actions

5. HTTP PROTOCOL
   └─> Page navigations send HTTP GET requests
   └─> Form submissions send HTTP POST requests
   └─> Backend responds with HTML/JSON


EXPECTED TEST EXECUTION TIME
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Step 1 (Open home):        2-5 seconds   (load website)
Step 2 (Browse):           1-2 seconds   (hover/click)
Step 3 (Select product):   2-3 seconds   (load product page)
Step 4 (Add to cart):      1-2 seconds   (click action)
Step 5 (Go to cart):       2-3 seconds   (load cart page)
Step 6 (Checkout):         2-3 seconds   (validate/navigate)
Step 7 (Login check):      1-2 seconds   (verify state)
        If NOT logged in:
        ├─ Step 7B.1: 2-3 seconds (signup page)
        ├─ Step 7B.2: 2-3 seconds (register)
        └─ Step 7B.3: 8-12 seconds (re-add product)
Step 8 (Checkout 2):       2-3 seconds   (repeat click)
Step 9 (Fill form):        1-2 seconds   (send keys)
Step 10 (Place order):     2-3 seconds   (submit)
Step 11 (Verify popup):    2-3 seconds   (switch to iframe)
Teardown:                  1-2 seconds   (quit browser)
                           ──────────────
TOTAL (if logged in):      25-45 seconds
TOTAL (if not logged in):  40-70 seconds


COMMON FAILURE SCENARIOS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. TIMEOUT EXCEPTION
   Problem: Element not found within 10-20 seconds
   Cause: Website slow, element not loading, wrong locator
   Solution: Increase wait time, check XPath in browser DevTools

2. STALE ELEMENT EXCEPTION
   Problem: Element was valid, then page refreshed
   Cause: Element reference becomes invalid after page reload
   Solution: Re-find element after page changes

3. NO SUCH ELEMENT EXCEPTION
   Problem: Element selector is wrong
   Cause: XPath/CSS selector doesn't match page HTML
   Solution: Update selector using browser inspector

4. ELEMENT NOT CLICKABLE EXCEPTION
   Problem: Element covered by overlay or disabled
   Cause: JavaScript dialog, modal popup blocking
   Solution: Use JavaScript click instead of regular click

5. ELEMENT NOT VISIBLE EXCEPTION
   Problem: Element exists but not displayed
   Cause: Hidden by CSS, below viewport, opacity 0
   Solution: Scroll to element, wait for visibility

6. CONNECTION TIMEOUT
   Problem: Browser can't reach website
   Cause: Website down, network issue, incorrect URL
   Solution: Check website status, network connection


DEBUGGING TIPS
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1. CHECK FAILURE SCREENSHOTS
   └─> reports/screenshots/*.png
   └─> Shows exact browser state when test failed

2. INCREASE WAIT TIMES
   └─> Change WebDriverWait(driver, 10) → (driver, 30)
   └─> If timeout, website is slow

3. ADD PRINT STATEMENTS
   └─> print(driver.current_url)
   └─> print(driver.page_source)
   └─> Run with: pytest -v -s (captures prints)

4. USE BROWSER INSPECTOR
   └─> Open Chrome DevTools (F12)
   └─> Use element inspector to find selectors
   └─> Copy XPath/CSS selector from DevTools

5. RUN WITH DETAILED OUTPUT
   └─> pytest tests/test_e2e_flow.py -v -s --tb=long
   └─> Shows all debug information

6. TAKE MANUAL SCREENSHOTS
   └─> driver.save_screenshot("debug.png")
   └─> Add in test code at any point to see state


ALLURE REPORT GENERATION
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

After test completion:

1. RUN COMMAND:
   └─> pytest --alluredir=reports/allure-results

2. DATA COLLECTED:
   ├─ Test name
   ├─ Execution time
   ├─ Pass/Fail status
   ├─ Error messages (if failed)
   ├─ Screenshots (if failed)
   └─ System information

3. GENERATE HTML REPORT:
   └─> allure serve reports/allure-results

4. REPORT SHOWS:
   ├─ Summary (total tests, passed, failed, duration)
   ├─ Test history (trends over time)
   ├─ Detailed results (each test)
   ├─ Failure details (errors, screenshots)
   ├─ Timeline (execution order)
   └─> Beautiful interactive HTML dashboard


